  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cooler air arrives today, with scattered rain \nshowers, then rain and snow showers possible through Tuesday morning.\n\nA surface low over northern Quebec, is dragging a cold front across \nthe region early this morning. Where spotty showers will soon fall \nacross far WNY due to waning moisture along front. This cold front \nwill cross the region from west to east and mainly remain as spotty \nshowers across WNY, with dry weather prevailing across areas east of \nRochester.\n\nThe cold front will exit east of the forecast area by dawn, \nsupporting colder air to advect across the region today and result \nin lake clouds and spotty precipitation east of the lakes. The \nstrong cold air advection will allow 850 mb temperatures drop to \nbetween -5C to -10C by nightfall. This will create enough lake \ninduced instability for a mix of rain and or snow (especially across \nthe higher terrain) lake effect showers southeast of both lakes. \nThis being said, while instability will be ample, moisture behind \nthe front will be quite limited, thus much of the lake response will \nbe light. However, the best chances for showers will be with the mid-\nlevel trough axis passing across the region overnight, providing an \nuptick in moisture.\n\nSurface high pressure will return and build overhead Tuesday, \nsupporting dry air to filter back into the region and cause lake \neffect precipitation to end by late Tuesday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonable temperatures persist throughout the week, \nwith increasing chances for cool rain arriving Thursday.\n\nA deep longwave trough will develop across the lower Mississippi \nValley mid-week, with phasing shortwaves within the jet stream \nproducing a negatively tilted mid-level trough over the southern \nAppalachians by Thursday. To the lee of the Appalachians and near \nthe Carolina coastline a surface low will develop, with a protruding \n1.5 PV anomaly down to near 650 hPa deepening a surface low as it \ntracks northward along the Atlantic Coastline.  \n\nAhead of this trough deeper Gulf moisture will surge northward, with \na widespread area of rain developing within height falls ahead of \nthe mid level trough, reaching our southern zones Thursday, and \nspreading northward through the afternoon and overnight hours. Model \nconsensus is increasing for precipitation probabilities with this \nsystem, with one-half of the ensemble members among the GFS, ECMWF \nand Canadian models depicting at least a half inch of rainfall with \nthis system. The lack of deep cold air will allow for much of the \nprecipitation to remain plain rain, with minor higher terrain snow \npossible later Thursday night and into Friday morning. \n\nA likely deformation band with this system will continue light \nshowers of rain and higher terrain snow showers through Friday and \ninto Friday night.\n\nStay tuned for how this system develops and tracks heading into the \nholiday weekend.",
